result.plain_answer:
Yes, with conditions. GMTrade only checks that the "owner" signed and matches the address it has on record. It never checks whether that owner is a normal wallet or a program address, so your vault program can own orders and positions by signing with its own program address. This already happens on mainnet: GMTrade's own treasury program, and at least one outside program ("Flipper", FQ8Uuy…), open, close and cancel GMTrade positions this way. GMTrade's keepers fill those orders within 4–8 seconds and pay USDC into accounts that only the program controls. The conditions are:
- use a separate, empty program address for each funded trader, topped up with about 0.05 SOL;
- never let the trader choose where proceeds go;
- run your own small bot that tells the vault when a fill has happened, because GMTrade will not notify your program (its "callback" feature is locked to GMTrade's own competition program);
- plan for payouts that are not USDC: profits on long trades can arrive in the market's other token (for example, wrapped SOL).

findings.claim:
Each owner PDA needs a small SOL float. Approximate costs:
- user account: 0.0045 SOL, once;
- position account: about 0.0056 SOL rent plus about 0.0225 SOL liquidation reserve, moved in on first use;
- each order: about 0.0245 SOL up front (order rent 0.0181, escrow token accounts, execution fee of at least 0.0003).
Most of this comes back to the owner when orders and positions close.

corrections_to_chat:
'Handle pending/cancelled orders' was right but incomplete:
- the keeper executes and closes the order in the same transaction (about 4–8 s);
- GMTrade will not call back into your program, because callbacks are allowlisted to GMTrade's competition program, so you need your own sync step;
- if your program address lacks a token account for an output token, the keeper's close is skipped and funds wait in escrow until your program closes the order itself.
